<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8" standalone="yes"?><rss version="2.0" xmlns:atom="http://www.w3.org/2005/Atom"><channel><title>5. Non-linear models and trees on SPM 405 — Machine Learning</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-05/</link><description>Recent content in 5. Non-linear models and trees on SPM 405 — Machine Learning</description><generator>Hugo</generator><language>en</language><atom:link href="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-05/index.xml" rel="self" type="application/rss+xml"/><item><title>Opening</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-05/opening/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-05/opening/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="opening"&gt;Opening&lt;a class="anchor" href="#opening"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-2"&gt;Today&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-2"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Part 1: polynomials and step functions; splines; generalised additive models. Part 2: decision trees and pruning; bagging and random forests; boosting&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Reading: ISLP Chapter 7 (7.1–7.4, 7.7) and Chapter 8 (8.1–8.2). ESL Chapters 5, 9.2, 10 and 15&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;ESL 5 covers splines, 9.2 trees, 10 boosting and 15 random forests, all in more mathematical depth.&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Notebook: lecture05_nonlinear_trees.ipynb on Google Classroom&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;h2 id="slide-3"&gt;From last week: your cross-validation errors&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-3"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Every model so far was linear in its inputs (plus products and powers we chose by hand). Today: models that find the shape themselves&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Beyond straight lines</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-05/beyond-straight-lines/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-05/beyond-straight-lines/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="beyond-straight-lines"&gt;Beyond straight lines&lt;a class="anchor" href="#beyond-straight-lines"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-5"&gt;Example 1: a building&amp;#39;s electricity demand&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-5"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="photo"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/photos/smart_meter.jpg" alt="A smart electricity meter records the demand every few minutes." width="460" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;A smart electricity meter records the demand every few minutes.&#10; &lt;span class="credit"&gt;Photo: RobbieIanMorrison, &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Logarex_smart_household_electricity_meter_build_year_2023.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Wikimedia Commons&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;CC BY 4.0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;figure class="slide-fig"&gt;&lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/fig/lecture-05/l05-609de586324b.svg" width="345" alt="Figure: Example 1: a building's electricity demand" loading="lazy" /&gt;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;A smart meter records the power drawn by an office building. Low at night. A morning peak (about 9 am). A larger evening peak (about 7:30 pm)&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;A load profile like this is what an energy manager or a utility wants to predict, for example to size a solar system or plan tariffs.&#10;Energy monitoring was a survey topic.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item><item><title>Tree-based methods</title><link>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-05/tree-based-methods/</link><pubDate>Mon, 01 Jan 0001 00:00:00 +0000</pubDate><guid>https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/lecture-05/tree-based-methods/</guid><description>&lt;h1 id="tree-based-methods"&gt;Tree-based methods&lt;a class="anchor" href="#tree-based-methods"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h1&gt;&#10;&lt;h2 id="slide-12"&gt;Example 2: six sensors on each machine&lt;a class="anchor" href="#slide-12"&gt;#&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/h2&gt;&#10;&lt;figure class="photo"&gt;&#10; &lt;img src="https://ndungundegwa.github.io/spm405-notes/photos/electric_motor.jpg" alt="Six sensors on each motor: vibration, temperature, current…" width="520" loading="lazy" /&gt;&#10; &lt;figcaption&gt;Six sensors on each motor: vibration, temperature, current…&#10; &lt;span class="credit"&gt;Photo: Rama, &lt;a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Electric_engine-MHS_1616-IMG_3859-gradient.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;Wikimedia Commons&lt;/a&gt;, &lt;a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/3.0/fr/deed.en" target="_blank" rel="noopener"&gt;CC BY-SA 3.0 fr&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&#10; &lt;/figcaption&gt;&#10;&lt;/figure&gt;&#10;&#10;&lt;table&gt;&#10;&#9;&lt;thead&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;th&gt;&lt;strong&gt;input&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;th&gt;&lt;strong&gt;unit&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/th&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&lt;/thead&gt;&#10;&#9;&lt;tbody&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;vibration&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;mm/s&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;bearing temperature&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;\(^\circ\)C&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;motor current&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;A&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;acoustic noise&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;dB&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;running hours&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;h&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;humidity&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;td&gt;%&lt;/td&gt;&#10;&#9;&#9;&#9;&lt;/tr&gt;&#10;&#9;&lt;/tbody&gt;&#10;&lt;/table&gt;&#10;&lt;blockquote class='book-hint '&gt;&#10;&lt;p&gt;Inputs: vibration (mm/s), bearing temperature (\(^\circ\)C), motor current (A), acoustic noise (dB), running hours (h), humidity (%)&lt;/p&gt;&#10;&lt;/blockquote&gt;&lt;p&gt;A typical condition-monitoring record. Motor current rises when a motor is overloaded or a bearing drags. Acoustic noise and running hours&#10;indicate wear. Humidity is included as an input that plays no part.&lt;/p&gt;</description></item></channel></rss>